## 1. Introduction to Logs

Logs are records of events generated by systems, applications, networks, and security devices.

They help SOC analysts monitor activities, investigate incidents, detect suspicious behavior, and troubleshoot problems.

**Why are logs important?**
- Monitor system and user activities.
- Detect suspicious behavior and security threats.
- Investigate security incidents.
- Identify unauthorized access.
- Maintain visibility across IT infrastructure.

## 2. Common Types of Logs

| Log Type | Common Sources | Typical Use Cases |
|---|---|---|
| System Logs | Windows Event Logs, Linux Syslog, macOS Logs | Startup/shutdown events, authentication, service status, hardware errors |
| Security Logs | Authentication Logs, Firewall, IDS/IPS, Antivirus, EDR, PAM | Login attempts, access control, malware detection, privilege escalation |
| Application Logs | Web Servers, Databases, Application Frameworks (IIS, Apache, Nginx) | Application errors, user activity, performance issues, debugging |
| Network Logs | Firewalls, Routers, Switches, NetFlow, Proxy, DNS | Traffic analysis, suspicious connections, data exfiltration, troubleshooting |
| Cloud Logs | AWS CloudTrail, Azure Activity Logs, GCP Audit Logs, SaaS Logs | User activity, resource changes, security monitoring, compliance audits |

